aliban contacts  before we came  just to say you know this is the  situation we we can't get back into new  zealand we can't be in qatar  um  will it be a problem if we come um and  and they said we're happy for you  congratulations  which  i mean it was a real gesture of um  of welcoming and and  empathy of you know we appreciate your  difficult situation come here and um  you know we we wish you the best  so so yes we're waiting here i mean the  new zealand government  i haven't heard anything directly from  them since they rejected us a week ago i  did get an email from a generic email  address just this morning saying we  welcome you to apply under a different  category  but the fact that remains is that they  told us to apply under kind of a medical  critical category um and now they're  saying something else which means  basically they're admitting that there  is no pathway  for a pregnant new zealand woman to  return to give birth um and  i i have so many questions about how how  we've got to this point and  and also kind of the the liability  around that is how how can they justify  it what what are the ethics and  reasoning behind it  especially as you know foreign athletes  foreign musicians um  there's uh the cricket world cup is in  new zealand right now with dozens of  foreign athletes  so how do they justify allowing foreign  citizens in for sporting events and  music events and not their own citizens  in  need of help  and you've got uh a home in afghanistan  um you've had kind words from the  taliban do you feel safe there what  would happen if you  couldn't get back to new zealand as it  seems you can't at the moment and things  don't change and you have to give birth  in afghanistan  yeah i mean i've done plenty of stories  over the years on the state of maternity  care here and it is  dire  um in october i went to a maternity  clinic they didn't have power they  didn't have medicine they couldn't do  c-sections  they had crumpled up some paracetamol in  a newspaper and said we've got this to  give to the woman but that'll run out  next month  um and i mean that's not even talking  about premature gear  so  it is incredibly dire for the woman uh  here and and i am really worried about i  mean i'm 25 weeks today um  i'm worried that if my child a little  girl comes early you know there really  won't be many options for treatment for  her um or myself for that matter if if  she's premature so  uh we really don't want to be here after  about 30 weeks and um  another country has come forward of  which neither of us are a citizen for my  partner and i saying we really feel for  you and can we explore visa options to  try and provide some type of stop gap  for you um in a place that does have  medical  treatment that's equivalent to new  zealand's  so yeah so we're kind of going down that  path as a stop gap and hoping in the  meantime that the new zealand government  um appreciates the needs of its its  citizens abroad  your plan you'll hopefully find another  country that will take you in with good  medical  with a good medical situation that's  literally your only option if new  zealand won't take you back  uh yeah  that's that's pretty much it and you  know  i  am in the media i have a lawyer um  you know i have um i guess resources to  speak out about this but this has been  going on for two years uh with new  zealanders forced to play a lottery to  get back in and then apply for emergency  exemptions if they don't win in  dangerous situations there are thousands  of new zealanders there's a million of  us overseas and and thousands of those  people as you can imagine over the last  two years have had  relatives die have been diagnosed with  terminal illnesses have had visas expire  and have had to go through the same  process that i'm going through now  uh to plead with the government please  let me back in  you know my mother is you know an  intensive care um i don't want her to  die and me not be there i i'